# Avell Storm 470 (Uniwill/TongFang barebone) — ITE HID controllers
# Grants non-root access to the per-key RGB keyboard controller and the
# chassis light bar.
#
# The 60- prefix is load-bearing, not cosmetic. TAG+="uaccess" only does
# anything if the tag exists by the time /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/73-seat-late.rules
# evaluates `TAG=="uaccess" ... RUN{builtin}+="uaccess"`. udev reads rule files
# in filename order and matches TAG== against the tags added so far in that
# pass, so this file must sort BEFORE 73-. It was 99- until 2026-09-01, which
# meant that match was always false, the builtin was never queued, and no ACL
# was ever set — the devices were reachable only through GROUP="input".
#
# That mattered on 2026-08-31, when Omarchy 4.0.2's migration 1787865477
# ("Drop the default input group grant, which allowed unprivileged keylogging")
# ran `gpasswd -d $USER input` and every avellcc call started failing with
# "open /dev/hidraw1: permission denied". Do not fix that by rejoining `input`:
# membership there also grants raw read/write on /dev/input/event*. uaccess
# grants only the user of the currently active seat, which is what we want.
#
# GROUP="root", MODE="0660", because uaccess is now the only intended path.
# The rule used to carry GROUP="input" as a fallback for system services and
# non-seat sessions; an audit on 2026-09-01 found no such consumer on this
# machine (the `input` group has zero members, no unit sets Group=input, and
# the user manager has Linger=no), so the fallback protected nothing while
# silently arming itself for whenever something else — a future ydotool or
# xpadneo install, per that migration's own carve-out — puts an account back
# in `input`. GROUP="root" grants nobody anything new: root already bypasses
# these checks.
#
# MODE stays 0660 and must. The obvious tightening — dropping to 0600, since
# only the ACL is supposed to matter — silently revokes the ACL instead of
# hardening it. POSIX ACL semantics make chmod set the *mask* from the group
# bits, so 0600 leaves `mask::---` and every named entry reads
# `user:disney:rw- #effective:---`: permission denied, with an ACL that still
# looks correct at a glance. Tried on 2026-09-01, and it broke exactly that
# way. Every uaccess-managed device on this system agrees — /dev/dri/card1,
# /dev/snd/controlC0 and the rest are all 0660 with mask=rw-.
#
# Only the two product ids present on this machine are listed. tuxedo-drivers'
# src/ite_8291_lb/ite_8291_lb.c names two more siblings for this protocol
# (0x7000, 0x6010) and avellcc's PIDs8233 still probes them, but they were
# never verified against real hardware or against any public id database, so
# shipping udev matches for them meant granting access to devices nobody has
# inspected. A machine that actually has one should add its line after checking
# that device's interface layout — see below for what to check.
#
# What to check before adding a product id: each of these controllers declares
# TWO interfaces — a boot-keyboard stub (bInterfaceClass 03, SubClass 01,
# Protocol 01) and the vendor interface the LEDs live on (03/00/00). This rule
# matches on vendor and product only, so it would grant access to every hidraw
# node the device exposes, keyboard interface included. That is safe here, and
# only here, because on both 048d:600b and 048d:7001 the boot-keyboard
# interface declares bNumEndpoints=00: usbhid cannot bind an interface with no
# endpoints, so it never gets a hidraw node and can never carry a keystroke.
# Verified on hardware 2026-09-01. On a controller where that interface has
# endpoints, narrow the rule with ENV{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM} (a property of the
# hidraw node itself) — note that ATTRS{bInterfaceProtocol} does NOT work here:
# `man udev` requires all ATTRS in one rule to match the same device, and
# idVendor lives on the USB device while bInterfaceProtocol lives on the
# interface. Matching on ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM also requires sorting after
# 60-persistent-hidraw.rules, which is what runs the usb_id builtin.

# ITE 8291 (rev 3 family) — keyboard RGB LED controller
SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{idVendor}=="048d", ATTRS{idProduct}=="600b", GROUP="root", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"

# ITE 8233 — chassis light bar (see docs/storm470-lightbar.md)
SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{idVendor}=="048d", ATTRS{idProduct}=="7001", GROUP="root", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"
